---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

const sections = [
  {
    title: "What is FloodWatch.PH?",
    body: "An open research project. It measures where floodwater actually reached during Philippine typhoons, and it runs a model that finds communities predicted to flood, but barely on record. Two tracks with two separate jobs, each reported with its own numbers. Open-source (MIT for code, CC-BY-4.0 for data).",
  },
  {
    title: "Why Sentinel-1 radar and not regular satellite photos?",
    body: "Regular optical satellites (Sentinel-2, Landsat, Planet) cannot see through the typhoon's own clouds. Sentinel-1 radar (it sees through typhoon clouds) sees the ground even during the storm. Optical photos during a Philippine typhoon would be almost entirely cloud. Sentinel-1 is the only sensor that works here, and we say so plainly.",
  },
  {
    title: "How accurate is the single-storm flood map?",
    body: "Track A (the single-storm flood map) was checked against an independent 2015 flood map (Tropical Storm Koppu / Lando, central Luzon). The overlap with that independent map is low and openly reported (see methodology). It uses an automatic cutoff with no hand-tuned settings, so there is nothing to bias the result toward a test set. The same method is then applied, without changes, to the Carina 2024 demo, which has no official measured record.",
  },
  {
    title: "How accurate is the flood-prediction model?",
    body: "Track B (the flood-prediction model) is tested by holding out whole storms, never random points (splitting on random points would inflate the score, because nearby points look almost identical). The precision and recall on that held-out test, and the full numbers, are on the <a class='text-accent-steel no-underline' href='/methodology'>methodology page</a>. A chart showing how well the score is calibrated is published with the project.",
  },
  {
    title: "What is the prediction-vs-record gap?",
    body: "It compares what the flood-prediction model (Track B) predicts for a province against what past records actually show (the Global Flood Database). It does not compare against the official hazard maps. Each province is sorted into one of four buckets. The headline one means predicted to flood, but barely on record: the model expects flooding, but there are few or no floods in the past record. That is not an accusation. Comparing against the official UP NOAH / PAGASA / MGB hazard maps is a later step, held back because those government maps are locked behind logins or apps and cannot be read as data yet. A city and barangay breakdown is also a later refinement. The language stays careful throughout.",
  },
  {
    title: "Does this expose anyone's personal information?",
    body: "No. Every figure for who and what is exposed (people and built-up land) is summed up by province only (a city and barangay breakdown is a later refinement). Population counts come from population data (WorldPop), rounded to the nearest 10. Built-up area comes from building footprints (Microsoft). No household-level shapes, no per-home flood status, no personal data of any kind. An automated check asserts this on every release. See <a class='text-accent-steel no-underline' href='/privacy'>/privacy</a> and the Privacy Impact Assessment.",
  },
  {
    title: "Why use Carina 2024 as the demo if it has no official flood map?",
    body: "That gap is exactly what the demo fills. No official flood map was released in time for Carina 2024 (Super Typhoon Gaemi plus an enhanced southwest monsoon, July 2024, Metro Manila). The checked Track A method is applied to Carina so communities have a satellite-based reference even when official records lag. The check event (Koppu 2015) has an independent flood map to compare against; the Carina demo has no official measured record, and we say so.",
  },
  {
    title: "What does 'rivers and lakes removed' mean and why does it matter?",
    body: "Radar sees rivers, lakes, the sea, and flood as the same thing: open water. Before we publish a flood map, we remove permanent water bodies (using public terrain and surface-water datasets plus OpenStreetMap water). What is left is flood, not the river that is always there. An automated check confirms the removal ran on every published map and that less than 5% of the mapped flood area overlaps permanent water.",
  },
  {
    title: "Can I use this data for journalism or research?",
    body: "Yes, under CC-BY-4.0. Cite as: <code class='text-sm'>FloodWatch.PH (2026), https://github.com/xmpuspus/floodwatch-ph</code>. In any report, describe it as 'flooding seen by Sentinel-1 radar, with rivers and lakes removed' rather than 'confirmed flooded areas.' The methodology page has a summary suitable for a press piece.",
  },
  {
    title: "Is this an official flood hazard map?",
    body: "No. FloodWatch.PH produces a satellite-based flood map and a statistical flood-prediction model. It is not an official hazard map and should not replace a formal engineering risk study. Its value is showing the gap between what the model predicts and what past records show; the official-hazard-map comparison is a later step. LGU planners and disaster coordinators should verify with local records before acting on these figures.",
  },
  {
    title: "Is FloodWatch.PH affiliated with PAGASA, NDRRMC, UP NOAH, or MGB?",
    body: "No. FloodWatch.PH is an independent open-source project by Xavier Puspus. Those agencies are named only as the intended source for the future official-hazard-map comparison; that is the only relationship, and that comparison is not in this version.",
  },
  {
    title: "How do I request takedown of a published feature?",
    body: "Open a <a class='text-accent-steel no-underline' href='https://github.com/xmpuspus/floodwatch-ph/issues/new?labels=takedown&template=takedown.md'>GitHub issue with the takedown label</a>, or email <a class='text-accent-steel no-underline' href='mailto:xpuspus@gmail.com?subject=Takedown%20request'>xpuspus@gmail.com</a>. Acknowledged within 5 working days; removed within 14 working days. For active doxxing concerns use the GitHub Security advisory form.",
  },
  {
    title: "I found a bug or have a contribution.",
    body: "Open an issue or PR on <a class='text-accent-steel no-underline' href='https://github.com/xmpuspus/floodwatch-ph'>github.com/xmpuspus/floodwatch-ph</a>. Most useful: verified floods we missed, province boundary corrections, official hazard-map sources for the future comparison, and cases where the prediction-vs-record gap looks wrong. See CONTRIBUTING.md.",
  },
];
---
<Base title="FAQ · FloodWatch.PH" pathname="/faq" description="Frequently asked questions about FloodWatch.PH: how accurate the flood map is, how it complies with RA 10173 (the Data Privacy Act), the prediction-vs-record gap, and the flood-prediction model.">
  <section class="max-w-prose mx-auto px-6 pt-10 pb-12">
    <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-ink-500 mb-3 font-mono">frequently asked</p>
    <h1 class="text-3xl mb-6 leading-tight text-ink-900 tracking-tight">Plain-language answers to the questions we hear most often.</h1>
    <p class="text-base text-ink-700 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      If your question isn't here, open a <a href="https://github.com/xmpuspus/floodwatch-ph/discussions" class="text-accent-steel no-underline">GitHub discussion</a> or email <a href="mailto:xpuspus@gmail.com" class="text-accent-steel no-underline">xpuspus@gmail.com</a>.
    </p>

    {sections.map((s, i) => (
      <article class={`py-5 ${i < sections.length - 1 ? "border-b border-ink-300/30" : ""}`}>
        <h2 class="text-lg text-ink-900 mb-2 leading-snug">{s.title}</h2>
        <p class="text-base text-ink-700 leading-relaxed" set:html={s.body} />
      </article>
    ))}

    <p class="text-[11px] text-ink-500 mt-10 pt-6 border-t border-ink-300/30">
      Observed flood extent derived from public satellite data (Copernicus Sentinel-1, AlphaEarth, GFD, MERIT Hydro, JRC GSW, WorldPop, Microsoft GlobalML Footprints, PSA/OSM). FloodWatch.PH computes statistical indicators derived from public data. Patterns may have legitimate explanations; figures warrant independent verification.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
